# Canvas GUI
@summary Retained-mode UI — vboxes, buttons, sliders, tabs, stateful do() closures
@category Canvas

CanvasGui is a tiny retained-mode UI toolkit. You declare your app state as a stateful type, then build a render() closure that returns a tree of GuiCmd nodes each frame. Event handlers are do() closures that capture this and mutate state in place; the render closure rebuilds the tree on the next tick.

## The shape of a CanvasGui app

stateful type AppState(...) — declare every piece of mutable UI state up front.

AppState(initial-values) { ... } — the block runs against a fresh heap-allocated instance. Inside, this refers to the instance.

let render = do() { guiRender(...) } — a do() closure that captures this. It is called every frame; rebuild the GuiCmd tree from current state.

guiWindow(title, width, height) — open the OS window.

guiRun(fps, render) — start the event loop, call render at the given rate, return when the user closes.

## Layout containers

guiVBox(children), guiHBox(children) — vertical / horizontal stack. Pass an array of GuiCmd.

guiGrid(rows, cols, children) — fixed-size grid.

guiTabs(active, labels, contents) — tab bar. Active is the current tab index; on(:select, ...) fires with the new index when the user clicks a tab.

guiScrollable(child) — wrap one child in a scrollable region.

guiSpacer(weight), guiMask(child) — flex spacing and clipping.

## Widgets

guiText(content) — plain or editable text. Editable mode is opt-in via .editable(true), then on(:change, do(s) { ... }) receives each new value.

guiButton(label) — clickable button. Use on(:click, do() { ... }) and optionally .selected(bool) to render it pressed (radio/toggle pattern).

guiSlider(min, max, value), guiDial(min, max, value) — value pickers. Both fire on(:change, do(v) { ... }) with the new float.

guiCanvas — a Canvas-backed drawing region inside the GUI tree.

## Style modifiers

GuiCmd values support a chain of style methods that return a new GuiCmd: .padding(units), .bg(r, g, b), .selected(bool), .editable(bool). These compose by chaining: guiText("x").padding(0.5).bg(0.14, 0.16, 0.22).

## Events

.on(:event, handler) attaches a handler to a node. Symbol literals like :click, :change, :select identify the event. The handler is a do() closure; the arity matches the event payload (zero args for :click, one for :change/:select).

--- example: counter.spn
-- The smallest stateful CanvasGui app: a counter with two buttons.
import CanvasGui
import String (formatNum)

stateful type AppState(counter: int)

AppState(0) {
  let render = do() {
    guiRender(
      guiVBox([
        guiText("Counter: " ++ formatNum(this.counter)),
        guiHBox([
          guiButton("−").on(:click, do() { this.counter = this.counter - 1 }),
          guiButton("+").on(:click, do() { this.counter = this.counter + 1 }),
        ]),
      ])
    )
  }

  guiWindow("Counter", 480, 200)
  guiRun(30.0, render)
}
---

--- example: text-input.spn
-- Editable text bound to this.name. Echo the current value above the field.
import CanvasGui

stateful type Form(name: string)

Form("hello world") {
  let render = do() {
    guiRender(
      guiVBox([
        guiText("Echo: " ++ this.name),
        guiText(this.name)
            .editable(true)
            .padding(0.5).bg(0.14, 0.16, 0.22)
            .on(:change, do(s) { this.name = s }),
      ]).padding(0.75)
    )
  }

  guiWindow("Text input", 520, 180)
  guiRun(30.0, render)
}
---

--- example: slider.spn
-- A slider and a dial both bound to floats.
import CanvasGui
import String (formatNum)

stateful type Knobs(volume: float, angle: float)

Knobs(0.5, 0.5) {
  let render = do() {
    guiRender(
      guiVBox([
        guiText("Volume: " ++ formatNum(this.volume)),
        guiSlider(0.0, 1.0, this.volume).on(:change, do(v) { this.volume = v }),
        guiText("Angle: " ++ formatNum(this.angle)),
        guiDial(0.0, 1.0, this.angle).on(:change, do(v) { this.angle = v }),
      ]).padding(0.75)
    )
  }

  guiWindow("Knobs", 480, 240)
  guiRun(30.0, render)
}
---

--- example: tabs-and-radio.spn
-- guiTabs switches sub-views; a radio group is implemented by binding
-- the .selected() state of each button to a comparison with this.choice.
import CanvasGui

stateful type Settings(tab: int, choice: _)

Settings(0, :red) {
  let render = do() {
    guiRender(
      guiTabs(this.tab, ["Colors", "About"], [
        guiVBox([
          guiText("Pick a color:"),
          guiHBox([
            guiButton("Red")
                .selected(this.choice == :red)
                .on(:click, do() { this.choice = :red }),
            guiButton("Green")
                .selected(this.choice == :green)
                .on(:click, do() { this.choice = :green }),
            guiButton("Blue")
                .selected(this.choice == :blue)
                .on(:click, do() { this.choice = :blue }),
          ]),
        ]).padding(0.75),
        guiVBox([
          guiText("This demo shows tabs and a radio-style button group."),
          guiText("Mutual exclusion is just `selected(this.choice == :foo)`."),
        ]).padding(0.75),
      ]).on(:select, do(i) { this.tab = i })
    )
  }

  guiWindow("Tabs + radio", 480, 280)
  guiRun(30.0, render)
}
---
